  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 218 AM EDT Saturday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: No significant weather expected today or tonight. Patchy \nearly AM fog may be locally dense in a few of the deeper river \nvalleys, but will generally dissipate with PBL mixing between 12-\n13Z. A narrow ridge of high pressure will bring mostly sunny and \nvery warm conditions to the North Country today. Will see an \nincrease in high cloud cover this afternoon associated with an \nupstream upper trough extending from northern Ontario southwestward \nto a closed 500mb low across Iowa. Even with filtered sunshine, \n850mb temperatures of +15C to +16C will support valley highs in the \nmid to upper 80s. Surface winds will become southerly at 5-10 mph \nsupported by weak southerly gradient flow. Modest low-level moisture \nadvection will generally keep 2-m dewpoints in the mid-60s, \nresulting in humid conditions areawide. \n\nFor tonight...generally quiet and muggy conditions. Inverted sfc \ntrough shifts ewd from the Ottawa Valley toward the St. Lawrence \nValley during the pre-dawn hours on Sunday. This will bring a low \nchance (20-30%) of some rain showers into the St. Lawrence Valley \nbefore daybreak. Otherwise, looking for overnight lows in the mid-\nupper 60s, except locally in the lower 60s east of the Green \nMountains.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: We'll be monitoring the slow-moving upper flow pattern \nSunday into Monday. Generally looking at a positive tilt mid-level \ntrough across sern Ontario, which extends back southwestward to a \nclosed low in the Ohio Valley region. Prevailing mid and upper level \nridge over Eastern New England will result in very slow movement to \nthis upstream system, but it appears that forcing will be sufficient \nfor isolated to scattered showers by Sunday afternoon, especially \nacross nrn NY. Weak mid-level lapse rates support limited \ninstability, with narrow CAPE profiles. However, increasing PW \nvalues, approaching 1.7-1.9\" and WBZ heights near 13kft will support \nlocally heavy rain with any convective activity that does develop. \n\nSupport for showers and thunderstorms evolves ewd across the \nChamplain Valley and across central/nrn VT during Sunday night into \nMonday. Have indicated high PoPs (80-90%) with widespread showers \nand a few embedded thunderstorms with weak CAPE through the \novernight hours. Again, thermodynamic conditions don't support a \nsevere threat, but locally heavy rainfall is possible. Sfc boundary \neventually settles south and east of our forecast area late Monday \nafternoon with generally drying conditions from NW-SE. At the \ncurrent time, have indicated 0.5 to 1.0\" rainfall with this system \nacross most of the North Country, and some amounts locally exceeding \n1.0\" in the St. Lawrence Valley. Can't rule out a localized flash \nflood threat, especially given slow movement of inverted trough and \nvery high PW values. However, not a strong consensus for heavy rain \noutside the St. Lawrence Valley at the present time, and amounts \nthere are currently not expected to be significant enough to produce \nflooding over the prevailing flatter terrain of the St. Lawrence \nValley. We will continue to monitor, but most sections of the North \nCountry will see a period of wetting rainfall late Sunday into \nSunday night and into the first half of Monday. Drier conditions \ngenerally return for Tuesday with highs in the low-mid 80s.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3: A typical late summer pattern looks to set up across the \nregion for the second half of the upcoming week. Daily chances for \nshowers, with some thunderstorms possible, seem likely as we see 250-\n500 J/kg of CAPE each afternoon with weak shear. This should result \nin pulse like showers and thunderstorms that try to develop across \nthe higher terrain and lake breezes. There is the potential for a \nround of organized convection towards the end of next week as a cut \noff low over the southeastern US phases with northern stream energy \nand ejects northward quickly ahead of a cold front. However, \ndeterministic guidance is all over the place with trying to resolve \nhow the southern stream and northern stream energy interact with one \nanother. Temperatures each afternoon should easily warm into the \nlower to mid 80s but given persistent cloud cover and shower \nactivity, it seems rather unlikely any location will hit 90 degrees \nthis week.",
